WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:12.643
to his autobiography, he was born on a bed made from a coffin that had once displayed the

2
00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:14.385
body of a distinguished count.

3
00:00:15.286 --> 00:00:22.032
He wrote, "Instead of a noble corpse here lay, on the second of April, 1805, a living and

4
00:00:22.092 --> 00:00:26.376
weeping child—that was myself, Hans Christian Andersen."

5
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:33.103
Although his readers might have doubted the story of his first bed, there is no question that Hans

6
00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:35.605
Christian Andersen came from a humble home.

7
00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:41.728
But, though born poor, Hans Christian Andersen's journey through life was a rich one.

8
00:00:42.689 --> 00:00:50.353
His fanciful imagination and his many experiences and adventures contributed greatly to his unique perception of the world.

9
00:00:51.474 --> 00:00:58.109
Andersen's way with words, coupled with his sensitive personality, molded him into one of the world's most loved

10
00:00:58.189 --> 00:00:59.550
writers of all time.

11
00:01:00.491 --> 00:01:07.014
Hans Christian Andersen was born in Odense, Denmark, where his father worked as a shoemaker and his mother

12
00:01:07.094 --> 00:01:08.615
washed other people's clothes.

13
00:01:10.216 --> 00:01:15.639
Andersen's father, though not formally educated, was literate and owned many books.

14
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:23.023
He read plays and Arabian folk tales to his young son, which instilled in the boy a lifelong

15
00:01:23.123 --> 00:01:27.482
love of storytelling and a respect for the power of the human imagination.

16
00:01:28.443 --> 00:01:35.710
At the time, Odense was the only city outside the capital, Copenhagen, that had a theater, and Andersen's

17
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.273
father took him now and then to see plays and operas.

18
00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:45.278
"The theater soon became my favorite place," Andersen wrote in his autobiography.

19
00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:51.944
But because of his poverty, the young boy could not attend performances as often as he wanted.

20
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.923
So he became friends with the man who handed out the playbills for each show, and the man

21
00:01:57.943 --> 00:01:59.924
would give Andersen one every day.

22
00:02:01.044 --> 00:02:07.207
The young boy would then sit on a street corner outside the theater, reading the playbill and imagining

23
00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:09.168
the entire play in his head.

24
00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:16.652
But Andersen's vivid imagination and sensitivity set him apart from the other children his age.

25
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.624
He was different from the boisterous boys who spent their time playing and fighting in the streets.

26
00:02:23.785 --> 00:02:29.691
Andersen wrote, "I very seldom played with other boys; even at school I took little interest in their

27
00:02:29.731 --> 00:02:36.677
games. At home I had playthings enough My greatest delight was in making clothes for my dolls."

28
00:02:37.858 --> 00:02:44.384
Andersen spent his time indoors reading stories and putting on plays for his dolls, who became his audience.

29
00:02:45.396 --> 00:02:50.520
Hans Christian Andersen's father died when he was 11, so the young boy had to work at a

30
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.641
textile factory to help his family.

31
00:02:53.702 --> 00:02:57.264
There, Andersen was constantly teased for being different.

32
00:02:58.325 --> 00:03:00.887
Other workers laughed at him and called him names.

33
00:03:02.028 --> 00:03:07.411
One day at work, some men grabbed Andersen and tried to pull down his pants to embarrass him

34
00:03:07.451 --> 00:03:08.332
in front of the others.

35
00:03:10.293 --> 00:03:15.553
Andersen managed to escape, but he felt so humiliated and hurt that he never went back.

36
00:03:16.674 --> 00:03:20.957
It wasn't long before Andersen concocted a better way to make money for his family.

37
00:03:21.978 --> 00:03:27.543
From the time he was very young, everyone had always loved his high soprano singing voice.

38
00:03:28.524 --> 00:03:32.947
When he would sing in his backyard garden, people would stop in the street and listen.

39
00:03:33.928 --> 00:03:38.552
Soon, the affluent people of Odense heard of this boy with the beautiful voice.

40
00:03:39.653 --> 00:03:44.613
They asked Andersen's mother to bring him to their homes to sing for them, and they paid him

41
00:03:44.693 --> 00:03:51.877
too. After a while, Hans Christian Andersen's mother saw that her son had enough money to become apprenticed

42
00:03:52.097 --> 00:03:52.717
to a tailor.

43
00:03:53.738 --> 00:04:00.182
But he didn't want to be a tailor; instead, he wanted to go to Copenhagen, the cultural center

44
00:04:00.262 --> 00:04:00.862
of Denmark.

45
00:04:01.803 --> 00:04:09.167
So, at the age of fourteen, the resolute boy arrived alone in Copenhagen determined to become the most

46
00:04:09.347 --> 00:04:10.888
famous performer in the city.

47
00:04:12.456 --> 00:04:19.920
It wasn't long before Andersen was singing for a party of wealthy guests, including Giuseppe Siboni, a famous

48
00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:22.662
tenor and the new director of the Royal Opera.

49
00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:29.806
After hearing Andersen, Siboni promised to help him develop his singing voice, and he took the young man

50
00:04:29.946 --> 00:04:30.647
into his home.

51
00:04:31.907 --> 00:04:40.508
Unfortunately, Siboni's support ended when Andersen's voice broke during a music lesson—his voice was starting to change—and his

52
00:04:40.588 --> 00:04:42.710
teacher advised him to learn a trade.

53
00:04:43.751 --> 00:04:48.654
Undaunted, Hans Christian Andersen did not abandon his desire to be a performer.

54
00:04:49.735 --> 00:04:55.960
He continued to write short plays and look for work until, by coincidence, his efforts were noticed by

55
00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:01.443
one of the directors of the Royal Danish Theater, an influential man named Jonas Collin.

56
00:05:02.444 --> 00:05:09.953
Collin, a philanthropist, recognized Andersen as an exceptional talent and decided to support the young man in his

57
00:05:10.013 --> 00:05:11.374
goal of becoming a writer.

58
00:05:12.374 --> 00:05:18.056
However, Andersen had little education, so Collin arranged for him to attend grammar school.

59
00:05:19.657 --> 00:05:26.140
By this time, Hans Christian Andersen was 17 years old and his fellow students were only 11 years

60
00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:31.162
old. Again, Andersen became the center of unwanted attention.

61
00:05:32.202 --> 00:05:40.856
Like the ugly duckling from his famous story of the same name, Andersen was oversized, clumsy, unappreciated, and

62
00:05:40.936 --> 00:05:42.157
out of place in this school.

63
00:05:43.178 --> 00:05:51.145
To remedy the situation, Collin arranged for private tutoring, and Andersen eventually graduated from Copenhagen University.

64
00:05:52.126 --> 00:05:59.092
Interestingly, even though he was burgeoning into a fine writer, Hans Christian Andersen had difficulty with spelling.

65
00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:06.493
Many people believe he had what is known today as dyslexia, a learning disorder that makes it difficult

66
00:06:06.533 --> 00:06:09.115
for a person to recognize some written words.

67
00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:16.019
Throughout his life, Andersen had to rely on others to check his stories for spelling errors, and he

68
00:06:16.099 --> 00:06:19.441
never mastered the more complicated writing style of the time.

69
00:06:20.801 --> 00:06:28.486
As a result, he developed his own style—more like the way ordinary people spoke—which was authentic and fresh.

70
00:06:29.526 --> 00:06:36.162
Later in life, despite his learning challenges, Andersen became fluent in English, Dutch, and German.

71
00:06:37.342 --> 00:06:42.485
During his university years, Andersen was publishing poetry as well as short stories.

72
00:06:43.565 --> 00:06:50.429
He received recognition and praise for his work, but it was not until 1835, when he published his

73
00:06:50.469 --> 00:06:56.672
first novel entitled The Improvisatore, or The Improviser, that he became a success.

74
00:06:57.728 --> 00:07:04.470
Hans Christian Andersen had grappled with adversity for a long time and suffered a lot of disappointment, but

75
00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:09.251
with this achievement, he hoped his years of anguish were finally coming to an end.

76
00:07:10.452 --> 00:07:15.693
Over time, Andersen became almost inseparable from his patron Collin and his family.

77
00:07:16.774 --> 00:07:21.775
They were from the very top of the upper class of Denmark, and the new writer began attending

78
00:07:21.855 --> 00:07:24.736
parties and socializing with the rich and powerful.

79
00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:34.287
He had gone from one extreme to another—from the poorest class to the most aristocratic level of society—but

80
00:07:34.327 --> 00:07:36.088
it was not a smooth transition.

81
00:07:37.209 --> 00:07:41.773
Andersen continued to feel awkward and never fully accepted by the upper class.

82
00:07:42.794 --> 00:07:48.239
He wrote of his emotions and struggles in another of his most famous stories, "The Little Mermaid."

83
00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:55.581
In this story, the main character leaves her underwater home and family to become part of a human

84
00:07:55.621 --> 00:07:59.122
world that is completely different, and she doesn't fit in.

85
00:08:00.243 --> 00:08:09.627
Hans Christian Andersen wrote in many forms—plays, novels, short stories, and travel essays—but his popularity exploded when he

86
00:08:09.687 --> 00:08:11.227
began writing fairy tales.

87
00:08:12.328 --> 00:08:20.431
Drawing on the stories and experiences from his childhood, Hans Christian Andersen identified with the unfortunate, the misunderstood,

88
00:08:20.852 --> 00:08:21.672
and the overlooked.

89
00:08:22.708 --> 00:08:29.630
His main characters were often ugly, and this attribute usually hid an inner beauty and strength that was

90
00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:32.171
later revealed after misfortune and struggle.

91
00:08:33.891 --> 00:08:42.353
Most importantly, his stories possessed the transcendent quality of appealing to children while conveying a moral lesson that

92
00:08:42.494 --> 00:08:43.974
even adults could appreciate.

93
00:08:44.974 --> 00:08:50.696
With the translation of his stories into many languages, Andersen became one of the most famous writers in

94
00:08:50.736 --> 00:08:56.009
the world, and later his life would be made into a hit film that was nominated for six

95
00:08:56.069 --> 00:08:58.230
Academy Awards in 1953.

96
00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:06.252
Hans Christian Andersen died on August 4, 1875, at age seventy after a long illness.

97
00:09:07.233 --> 00:09:11.374
But his fairy tales, with their unforgettable characters, live on.

98
00:09:11.394 --> 00:09:21.283
"Thumbelina," "The Ugly Duckling," "The Snow Queen," "The Little Match Girl"—these stories and many more have allowed readers

99
00:09:21.343 --> 00:09:26.407
to see some of Andersen's own joy and suffering revealed in every tale.
